  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1145 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air analysis late \nTuesday evening reveal weak ridging is in place from the southern \nplains through the central plains with a significant, cut-off trough \ncentered just offshore off the west coast. At the surface, broad\nhigh pressure reaches from its center over southern Quebec, \nCanada, southwestward through the central plains, resulting in \nmodest southerly winds across southwest KS. For the remainder of\nthe overnight period, 10-15 mph winds and partly to mostly \ncloudy skies will support Wednesday morning lows in the low to \nmid 50s. Daytime Wednesday, short range guidance indicates the \nstrong cut-off low out west will make little progress eastward \nas the downstream ridge amplifies considerably, extending \nthrough the northern plains and into south-central Canada. As a \nresult, surface high pressure over our area will be reinforced, \nyielding light and variable winds and a slight uptick in \nafternoon temperatures with highs in the mid to upper 70s. \nWednesday night, light winds and mostly clear skies will permit \nlows dropping into the upper 40s to low 50s.\n\nVery little change to the synoptic pattern is forecast on Thursday \nas the high amplitude trough out west and the ridge overhead inch \neastward. Nevertheless, lee cyclogenesis will begin in east-central \nMT and adjacent areas, which will engender more robust southerly \nwinds across southwest KS in the 15-25 mph range gusting to 30-35 \nmph. Additionally, 850-mb temperatures are progged to increase by 2-\n4 degrees C, which will translate to afternoon highs reaching the \nupper 70s/low 80s.\n\nFriday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles agree \nthe strong upper wave will stall out over the central Rockies before \na shortwave trough diving southeast from south-central Canada pushes \nit onto the High Plains over the weekend and into early next week. \nThis synoptic evolution will support a steady, moderate cooling \ntrend as afternoon temperatures fall from the upper 70s on Friday to \nthe upper 50s/low 60s Tuesday and beyond. Furthermore, the slow \nprogression of the upper wave is ideal for precipitation across \nsouthwest KS, and this is indeed reflected by the NBM, which paints \nplenty of chance (25-54%) to likely (55-74%) pops across much of our \narea Saturday and Sunday, as well as the ECMWF ENS and GEFS mean \ntotal precipitation fields which are in the 1-2\" and 0.75-1.5\" \nrange, respectively, through next Monday evening.",
